WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.960
Rockets are magnificent engineering feats that&nbsp;
have allowed us to travel into space and orbit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:10.560
over the past several decades. But, though rockets&nbsp;
are extremely cool, they cost massive amounts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:15.520
money and carry substantial risk during each&nbsp;
flight. In contrast, we have our other flying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:21.600
masterpiece: the airplane. Not only can airplanes&nbsp;
also fly, but they’re astronomically cheaper and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:26.720
more reliable than rockets. In fact, the annual&nbsp;
risk of an American being killed in an airplane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:32.800
crash is only 1 in 11 million. To put that into&nbsp;
perspective, Nasa estimates that there’s a 1 in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:39.440
276 chance of a Falcon 9 disaster leading to loss&nbsp;
of life. That means that one of the most reliable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:46.160
rockets of all time is still 40,000 times riskier&nbsp;
than your average plane. But let’s be real here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:51.360
I’m sure the brainiacs working at space agencies&nbsp;
around the world have already considered this. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:58.160
why can’t planes just fly into space? Well,&nbsp;
to answer this question, we have to first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:02.480
understand the physics behind how airplanes fly&nbsp;
in the first place. While it may seem like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:07.840
powerful engines on planes are doing all the work&nbsp;
in keeping us airborne, this is actually not true.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:12.960
In fact, the engines on airplanes are quite&nbsp;
weak in comparison to the maximum capacity of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:17.680
the plane. The ratio between the force generated&nbsp;
by a plane’s engines and the gravitational force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:22.240
pulling down the airplane is called the thrust to&nbsp;
weight ratio. And you’ll be surprised to hear how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:29.520
low these ratios really are. The Boeing 747-8&nbsp;
only has a thrust to weight ratio of 0.269,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:35.680
and the largest passenger aircraft, the airbus&nbsp;
a380, only has a thrust to weight ratio of 0.227.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:41.280
The thrust to weight ratios of most&nbsp;
commercial aircraft land between 0.2 and 0.35.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:45.600
Even the concorde, which was a supersonic&nbsp;
commercial aircraft, only has a thrust to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:51.760
weight ratio of 0.372. But, if airplane engines&nbsp;
are only generating enough thrust to lift a fifth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:56.960
to a third of the plane's maximum weight, how&nbsp;
is the plane even able to gain altitude? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:01.280
this is where extremely clever engineering of&nbsp;
the wings comes into play. The underside of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:06.720
airplane’s wings are flat while the topside of the&nbsp;
airplane’s wings are rounded. This means that air&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:11.040
flowing on the top side of the wing has to travel&nbsp;
a further distance than the air on the bottom side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:16.240
of the wing. The total volume of air flowing on&nbsp;
either side of the wing, however, is the same. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:20.480
the air on the top side of the wing is spread over&nbsp;
a larger area than the air on the bottom side of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:24.960
the wing. This difference causes the top side of&nbsp;
the wing to have a lower pressure than the bottom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:29.840
side of the wing. Given that particles naturally&nbsp;
want to move from high pressure to lower pressure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:34.640
the wing naturally wants to move upwards, and&nbsp;
this force is called lift. If we’re able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:38.640
create a large enough pressure difference between&nbsp;
two sides of the wings, the generated lift will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:42.720
be greater than the gravitational force pulling&nbsp;
down the airplane, and that’s the secret behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:47.600
how airplanes actually gain altitude. Over the&nbsp;
past several decades, engineers have basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:52.720
perfected every single component of the plane&nbsp;
from bending the tips of wings to utilizing flaps.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:58.080
All of these efforts have one goal in mind,&nbsp;
maximize lift and minimize fuel consumption.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:02.560
While this has made airplanes insanely efficient,&nbsp;
it has also made them extremely dependent on air.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:07.600
Without air, most of our planes wouldn’t&nbsp;
leave the ground. But what if we just put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:12.160
more powerful engines onto our planes or made&nbsp;
the planes themselves smaller. If we could get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:16.560
the thrust to weight ratio to be greater than&nbsp;
1, airplanes wouldn’t need lift to gain altitude&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:21.840
right? Well, that’s true, and we actually have&nbsp;
a bunch of fighter jets that do have a thrust to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:28.640
weight ratio that’s greater than 1. The F-15, for&nbsp;
instance, has a ratio of 1.04 and the F-22 has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:34.720
ratio 1.09 which can even be increased to 1.26&nbsp;
with the right combination of fuel and payload.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:41.040
This is why these jets can do vertical takeoffs,&nbsp;
loops, barrel rolls, and who knows what else. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:45.280
just because these jets are not dependent on&nbsp;
lift doesn’t mean that they aren’t dependent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:50.800
on air. And that brings me into the fundamental&nbsp;
difference between rocket engines and jet engines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:55.600
Both engines use combustion and Newton’s third&nbsp;
law to create thrust, but if you think about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:59.120
you’d quickly realize that a jet engine&nbsp;
has both in intake and an exhaust&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:03.840
while rocket engines only have an exhaust. Jet&nbsp;
engines have an intake because they rely on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:09.600
oxygen in the air for combustion. Without a steady&nbsp;
intake of oxygen, combustion can’t take place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:15.440
and the jet engines simply won't work. Now, it’s&nbsp;
not like rocket engines are any better. Rocket&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:20.720
engines also need oxygen to work, and this is&nbsp;
why we have solid and liquid rocket propellant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:25.520
In a solid fuel rocket, the fuel and oxidizer&nbsp;
is mixed together into a chemical and engines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:30.240
simply use the chemical during flight. With&nbsp;
liquid fuel rockets, the fuel and the oxidizer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:34.320
are actually stored separately and the engines&nbsp;
combine the two ingredients during combustion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:39.280
But either way, both jet and rocket&nbsp;
engines need oxygen to operate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:43.840
and since jet’s don’t carry oxygen along&nbsp;
with them, they can’t just fly into space.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:49.200
So, the bottom line is basically just a lack of&nbsp;
atmosphere in space. Planes need the atmosphere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:53.040
to generate lift and they need the oxygen&nbsp;
in the atmosphere to fire up their engines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:57.520
If the atmosphere didn’t thin out as we reach&nbsp;
space, planes should theoretically be able to fly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:03.520
anywhere in the solar system, though it would be&nbsp;
a rather slow mode of transport. But, since that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:08.880
not the case, airplanes have a service ceiling&nbsp;
which is generally around 42 to 43,000 feet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:13.280
What if we just strapped rocket engines to&nbsp;
airplanes though, as long as the thrust to weight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:17.360
ratio is greater than 1, the aircraft would&nbsp;
no longer be dependent on the atmosphere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:22.178
People have actually had this idea for nearly a&nbsp;
hundred years at this point with the first rocket&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.178 --> 00:05:28.240
powered flight dating back to the early 1900s.&nbsp;
In 1928 a German plane the Lippisch Ente became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:34.080
the first aircraft to fly using rocket power. A&nbsp;
couple of years later in 1931, an Italian aviator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:39.022
named Ettore Cattaneo built the first privately&nbsp;
built rocket plane. We also saw several rocket&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.022 --> 00:05:43.520
powered aircraft throughout World War 2, but it&nbsp;
wasn’t until the cold war that we actually tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:48.800
to use this technology to reach space. The most&nbsp;
notable spaceplane attempt of all time was likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:54.880
the North American X-15. The X-15 was proposed&nbsp;
way back in December of 1954 which was before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.880 --> 00:06:00.880
Nasa even existed, so the plane was to be built by&nbsp;
North American Aviation and Reaction Motors. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:06.880
plan was to carry the X-15 on a mother ship called&nbsp;
the B-52 to an altitude of 8.5 miles at a speed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:13.600
of 500 miles per hour. At this point, the B-52&nbsp;
would release the X-15, and the X-15 would use two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:19.280
liquid propellant rocket engines to fly as high&nbsp;
as possible. Fortunately, the X-15 actually made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:23.920
it past the concept board, and the first rocket&nbsp;
powered flight would take place on September 17,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:30.160
1959. Over the next decade the various&nbsp;
versions of the X-15 would be flown 199 times.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:36.800
Fun fact, one of the pilots of the X-15 was Neil&nbsp;
Armstrong. Anyway, in 1963, pilot Joe Walker would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:42.080
successfully fly the plane past 100 km in altitude&nbsp;
which meant that the X-15 could officially reach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:47.040
space. But, aside from technically reaching space,&nbsp;
the X-15 couldn’t actually complete anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:52.800
meaningful in space. It couldn’t reach orbit and&nbsp;
it was nowhere near escape velocity of mach 33.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:59.120
But, nonetheless, the plane was extremely&nbsp;
impressive as it could reach mach 6 or 4000 mph,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:04.320
and it was a great first step in developing a&nbsp;
plane that could actually travel into space.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:08.800
After the X-15 program came to a close, Nasa&nbsp;
decided to basically develop a hybrid space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:13.600
plane with the space shuttle. The idea was to&nbsp;
launch like a rocket but land like a plane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:18.960
but this is when Nasa would run into by far the&nbsp;
largest obstacle when it comes to space planes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:23.360
and that’s excessive heat. Though the&nbsp;
X-15 was an extremely fast aircraft,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:27.280
it could only reach 1/5th the speed of&nbsp;
escape velocity. Speeding up the aircraft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:33.840
to escape velocity was extremely hard, but that&nbsp;
wasn’t the main obstacle. The main problem was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:39.520
that it was and still is nearly impossible to&nbsp;
handle the generated heat. But wait a minute,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:45.040
we have rockets that go way faster than the X-15,&nbsp;
so how are they able to handle the heat? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:49.760
that’s the reason that rockets launch vertically&nbsp;
at least at the start. The idea is to get the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:54.320
rocket as high as possible before it reaches&nbsp;
extreme velocities. At these higher altitudes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:59.040
the atmosphere will be much thinner which heavily&nbsp;
reduces the generated heat. The easy solution to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:03.280
this problem would be to just launch the space&nbsp;
plane vertically, and that’s basically what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:08.960
Nasa settled on with the Space Shuttle. At that&nbsp;
point, it’s more of a rocket than a space plane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:13.680
but even after doing this, Nasa faced major&nbsp;
heat problems when it came to reentry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:19.440
The underside of the space shuttle had a total&nbsp;
of 20,548 heat shield tiles, and every single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:24.320
tile had to be individually inspected after every&nbsp;
single flight. Even a small amount of damage to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:29.600
these heat shield tiles could lead to catastrophic&nbsp;
results like we saw with Columbia. So, truthfully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:34.160
Nasa never figured out an efficient way to refly&nbsp;
the Space Shuttle, and this was one of the biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:39.120
downfalls of the space shuttle. Considering this,&nbsp;
Nasa should’ve never tried to make a full on space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:43.680
plane until they figured out a more efficient&nbsp;
solution for the Space Shuttle, but nonetheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:48.240
Nasa started to work on a single stage to&nbsp;
orbit space plane called the X-30 in the 1980s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:52.880
The plane was slated to have top speeds of Mach 25&nbsp;
and it seemed like the plane would be straight out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:57.680
of movie. That should’ve been a major red flag&nbsp;
though as the plane was way too overambitious.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:03.520
According to Nasa engineer Ivan Bekey, the X-30&nbsp;
was “the biggest swindle ever to be foisted on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:08.640
country” and it “was full of dubious aerodynamic&nbsp;
claims and engine performance claims and thermal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:13.840
claims.” Basically, Nasa had nowhere near&nbsp;
enough technology to make the plane a reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:19.680
and the $1.7 billion invested into the project&nbsp;
was a complete waste. Nasa didn’t give up after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:25.440
the X-30 though. Later on, they started working&nbsp;
on the X-43 which did reach production and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:30.960
was able to achieve a top speed of Mach 9.6.&nbsp;
But even that isn't nearly enough to reach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:35.280
orbit using a space plane. In the meantime, the&nbsp;
overarching space community has come to a broad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:39.360
conclusion. What’s even the point of trying&nbsp;
to solve this nearly impossible heat problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:43.840
with space planes when we can just keep using&nbsp;
traditional rockets and continue exploring space.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:48.880
At the end of the day, who cares if the&nbsp;
vehicle launches vertically or horizontally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:53.760
the destination is what really matters. Though&nbsp;
it would be super cool to fly a plane into space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:58.160
it really doesn’t pose any additional benefits.&nbsp;
Originally, many scientists thought that space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:02.960
planes were the key to reusability and cheap&nbsp;
launch costs. But, SpaceX is proving that these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:07.280
attributes can be achieved using traditional&nbsp;
rockets, so the excitement around space planes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:12.560
is more or less dying off. Maybe a hundred years&nbsp;
from now after we’ve mastered traditional rockets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:16.000
we’ll see space planes make a&nbsp;
comeback. But in the meantime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:20.480
it makes no sense to try to battle with the laws&nbsp;
of physics when you can just launch vertically.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:25.760
Would you prefer to ride in a space plane or a&nbsp;
traditional rocket? Comment that down below. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

